            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0929" n="803" xml:id="pg803"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.00/105: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-07-29T20:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 29, 1925—8
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 29—3:35
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>300. Your telegram number 170 July 28, 2 p.m. 1. I feel very strongly that phrase
                    “tariff autonomy” ought not to be used [garbled group]. As the sentence is
                    phrased the omission suggested by you would seem to imply opening Special
                    Conference for the reconsideration of all treaty matters including such
                    questions as missionary rights and the surrender of concessions and leased
                    territories which the interested powers would not of course consent to discuss.
                    To make your meaning entirely definite might I not substitute the words “treaty
                    provisions dealing with customs duties” for “looking toward ultimate tariff
                    autonomy.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
